<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: East and Southern Africa</h1><p class="ws-lede">Highland Christian kings, Swahili merchant towns, plateau gold and the kingdoms that faced Portugal and Britain.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/east-and-southern-africa">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Aksumite Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Kongo Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Zulu Kingdom</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mutapa Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Kingdom of Mapungubwe</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Atlantic slave trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Chiefdom</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Dry-stone construction</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Gold trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Ivory trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Aksumite Empire</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Aksum grew rich trading ivory and gold through its Red Sea port of Adulis, and under King Ezana became one of the first states to adopt Christianity.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Kingdom of Zimbabwe</b>: &#8220;A Shona kingdom rich on cattle and on gold and ivory sent to Sofala, whose dry-stone walls are the largest precolonial stone structures in southern Africa.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Zulu Kingdom</b>: &#8220;Shaka turned a small chiefdom into the strongest state in southeastern Africa from 1816, and in 1879 the Zulu routed a British force at Isandlwana.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Highland Christian kings, Swahili merchant towns, plateau gold and the kingdoms that faced Portugal and Britain.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/east-and-southern-africa. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: East and Southern Africa</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Aksumite Empire <span>from 100 CE</span></li><li>Kingdom of Mapungubwe <span>from 1220 CE</span></li><li>Kongo Empire <span>from 1390 CE</span></li><li>Mutapa Empire <span>from 1430 CE</span></li><li>Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba <span>born c. 1583 CE</span></li><li>Zulu Kingdom <span>from 1816 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Atlantic slave trade</dt><dd>The Atlantic slave trade was the forced transportation of millions of enslaved Africans to the Americas between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries.</dd><dt>Chiefdom</dt><dd>The chiefdom was the form in which many farming and fishing peoples first lived under permanent rulers: a hereditary chief at the head of ranked kin, fed by tribute and upheld by ritual rather than by officials.</dd><dt>Dry-stone construction</dt><dd>Dry-stone construction is a building technique in which walls are made from carefully fitted stones without mortar.</dd><dt>Gold trade</dt><dd>The gold trade refers to the commerce in West African gold, often exchanged for Saharan salt and carried across the desert to North Africa and the Mediterranean.</dd><dt>Ivory trade</dt><dd>The ivory trade is the commerce in elephant tusks, prized for carving and luxury goods.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
